<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Marmaridae</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 400 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Libyan peoples of Marmarica, the arid coastland between Egypt and Cyrenaica, known from Greek and Roman writers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marmarica">Marmarica</a>, the stretch of coast and desert between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a>, was home to Libyan pastoral groups whom Greek and Roman writers called the Marmaridae from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>. They were probably descendants of earlier peoples such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/libu">Libu</a> and lived by herding, small-scale farming and oasis trade.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They were organised in clans and tribes rather than a single state. They traded with the coastal towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraetonium">Paraetonium</a> and served as guides and auxiliaries. Under Roman rule they were tributaries of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, but they raided settled lands when they could; Roman commanders, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-sulpicius-quirinius">Publius Sulpicius Quirinius</a> under Augustus, campaigned against them.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the late Roman period the name Marmaridae faded as new groups, such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/austuriani">Austuriani</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/laguatan">Laguatan</a>, dominated the Libyan desert. Their descendants were absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> and later Arab tribes of the region.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/marmaridae.jpg" alt="Marmaridae" width="320" height="262"><figcaption>Image: Sebastian Münster · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:M%C3%BCnster_Aphricae_Tabula_III_1540_UTA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 400 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber (Libyco-Berber)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>, <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Marmarica tribes</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=50&amp;focus=marmaridae" aria-label="Marmaridae on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5165 1193 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Marmaridae lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5485 1449h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5485 1449h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Libya. <a href="/atlas?year=50&amp;focus=marmaridae">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

